---
/**
 * Neighbourhood intent — targets "boat management Balboa Island".
 *
 * This is NOT the Lido Isle page with the name swapped. Google classifies near-
 * duplicate location pages as doorway pages and discounts them, and that is the
 * default failure mode of programmatic local SEO. Everything below is true of Balboa
 * Island specifically: residential dockage, no marina staff, constrained access.
 */
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Placeholder from '../components/Placeholder.astro';
import { url } from '../config';

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'Do you service boats at private residential docks on Balboa Island?',
    a: 'Yes. Most Balboa Island dockage is residential rather than marina dockage, and it is a large part of what we do. We arrange and attend vendor visits so the owner does not need to be home for them.',
  },
];
---

<Base
  title="Boat Management on Balboa Island"
  description="Boat management for Balboa Island residents. We service vessels at private residential docks and handle the trades, the schedule and the paperwork."
  path="/boat-management-balboa-island"
  heading="Boat management on Balboa Island"
  kind="location"
  place="Balboa Island"
  faq={faq}
>
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="shell">
      <p class="eyebrow">Balboa Island, Newport Beach</p>
      <h1>Boat management on Balboa Island.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Island dockage is residential — your boat lives behind your house, not in
        a marina with a dockmaster. That changes what management has to cover.
      </p>
      <a class="btn btn--primary" href={url('/contact')}>Request a Consultation</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell prose">
      <p class="eyebrow">The practical difference</p>
      <h2>Private dock, no marina staff.</h2>
      <p>
        On Balboa Island there is no marina office to accept a delivery, no
        dockmaster to let a technician in, and no yard on site. Every vendor visit
        needs someone to arrange access and be there for it. The dock itself —
        pilings, hardware, dock box, shore power — is part of what has to be
        maintained.
      </p>
      <p>
        That is the practical difference between managing a boat here and managing
        one in a slip. It is also why "I will just call someone when it needs
        something" tends to quietly stop happening.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="shell prose">
      <p class="eyebrow">Scope</p>
      <h2>What we handle for island dockage.</h2>
      <ul class="ticks">
        <li>Coordinating and attending every vendor visit, so you do not have to be home for it</li>
        <li>Regular in-person checks of the vessel, its lines, covers and shore power</li>
        <li>Dock-side condition monitoring alongside the vessel maintenance schedule</li>
        <li>The full maintenance schedule, compliance tracking and owner reporting that every managed vessel gets</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="notice">
        <p>
          <strong>Moving the vessel to and from the yard:</strong>
          <Placeholder
            key="terms"
            label="whether we provide vessel movement / captain services ourselves or coordinate a third party — CEO to confirm"
          />
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell prose">
      <p class="eyebrow">Access</p>
      <h2>Getting around the island.</h2>
      <p>
        Access matters here in a way it does not elsewhere in the harbor. The
        island's streets and alleys are narrow, parking is limited, and much of the
        practical access to a vessel is from the water side. We plan vendor visits
        accordingly rather than discovering the problem on the day.
      </p>
      <p>
        Also serving <a href={url('/boat-management-lido-isle')}>Lido Isle</a> and the wider
        harbor — see <a href={url('/yacht-management-newport-beach')}>yacht management in Newport Beach</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
